# Repo That Talks Back

Turn any GitHub repository into an interactive assistant that answers questions
with **file + line citations**, and can generate PR diffs for small changes.

## What makes it different

- **Citations or refusal.** Every answer includes `file:start-end` citations
  grounding it in the actual codebase. If the evidence is insufficient, the
  system explicitly refuses instead of hallucinating.
- **Runs for free.** No paid APIs. Local inference is optional (stub adapter
  included for future LLM integration). Works fully offline after cloning.
- **Offline index.** The JSONL index is stored locally -- no cloud dependencies.
- **GitHub URL support.** Pass a GitHub URL and it shallow-clones into a local
  cache (`--depth 1`). Cached clones are reused automatically.
- **Explain Repo.** One command to get a structured overview: what it is, how
  to run it, key directories, entry points, architecture graph, and a
  suggested reading order -- all with citations.
- **Patch generation.** Ask it to add logging or TODOs and get a clean unified
  diff you can review and apply.

## Quickstart

```bash
# Clone and install
git clone https://github.com/your-org/repo-that-talks-back.git
cd repo-that-talks-back
pip install -e ".[dev]"

# Index a local repo
python -m rtalk index --repo . --out .rtalk/index.jsonl

# Index a GitHub repo (shallow clone)
python -m rtalk index --repo https://github.com/owner/repo --out .rtalk/index.jsonl

# Re-clone (bypass cache)
python -m rtalk index --repo https://github.com/owner/repo --out .rtalk/index.jsonl --refresh

# Ask a question
python -m rtalk ask --index .rtalk/index.jsonl "How does the retrieval engine rank results?"

# Get a full repo overview
python -m rtalk explain --index .rtalk/index.jsonl

# Start the web UI
python -m rtalk serve
# Then open http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

## Demo

Run the included demo script:

```bash
bash scripts/demo.sh
```

This indexes the current repo, generates an overview, runs a sample query, and
prints results with citations.

